      <LC_LandCoverClass id="68" uuid="2bd0d900-6a09-11ec-ba4e-a86daa922d76" xsi:type="LC_LandCoverClass">
         <name>Mangrove </name>
         <description>Mangroves are intertidal forests in tropical and sub-tropical zones, occupying sheltered coasts and places with little influence of tidal energy. Just over 70 species of mangroves are recognized globally, including true mangroves (about 42 species) and associated species (about 32 species). In Mozambique, this type of training consists of essentially by the following species: Avicennia marina (white mangrove), Bruguiera gymnhoriza (red mango), Tagal ceriops (white mangrove), Coastal Heritiera (Mozambique mangrove), Lumnitzera racemosa (black sleeve), Rhizophora mucronata (red mangrove), Alba Sonneracy (mangrove apple) and Xylocarpus granatum (cannon ball mangal).  </description>
         <map_code>11</map_code>
         <elements>
            <LC_HorizontalPattern id="69" uuid="f0b14030-6a0d-11ec-ba4e-a86daa922d76" xsi:type="LC_HorizontalPattern">
