      <LC_LandCoverClass id="78" uuid="28889660-6a0a-11ec-ba4e-a86daa922d76" xsi:type="LC_LandCoverClass">
         <name>Mountain forest</name>
         <description>The mountain forest ranges from 1200 to 1600 m in altitude with rainfall from 1,700 to 2000 mm per year or in places where there is a sudden change in altitude (300 to 2500 m). These forests occur in the regions of Chimanimani (Moribane), Gurué, Milange, Vumba, Gorongosa, Namuli, Tamassa, Mueda plateau and among other highlands. The transition from low-altitude forests to Mountain forests occurs continuously, with a progressive replacement of species and the determining factor for the site is the high air humidity. In terms of composition, these forests have a lower specific diversity compared to those at low altitude. Typical species are:Widdringtonia whytei, Acacia abyssinica subsp, calophylla, Podocarpus spp., Celtis gomphophylla, Maesa lanceolata, Tabernaemontana stapfian, Curtisia dentata, Macaranga spp, Bombax rhodognaphalon, Diospyros mespiliformis, Cussonia spicata, Milicia excelsa, Cordyla africana, Kigelia africana, Newtonia buchananii, Ficus spp., Uapaca spp.,Vitex spp.</description>
         <map_code>13</map_code>
         <elements>
            <LC_Characteristic id="79" uuid="b0a53ef4-50f6-11de-bcd7-000cf147c442" xsi:type="LC_LandForm">
               <name>Land Form</name>
               <description>Describe the land form</description>
               <type>Mountainous Highland</type>
               <slope_class_type>Steeply Dissected to Mountainous</slope_class_type>
            </LC_Characteristic>
            <LC_Characteristic id="7A" uuid="b0a53ef5-50f6-11de-bcd7-000cf147c442" xsi:type="LC_TopographicalAspects">
